# Introduction

I was recently playing with around with [flutter web](https://flutter.dev/multi-platform/web) an was wondering how I would go about getting it to work with [Django]() and deploying them together.
The application I decided to test this with is a kanban type application, so flutter would be used to build the UI and django would  provide the REST API.


# Development

## Directory Structure
Our directory structure will look very similar to the traditional django project structure

```
kanban/
    apps/
	db.sqlite3
	flutter/
	    build/
	    pubspec.yaml
		web/
		....
	kanban/
	manage.py
```
So as you can see we have placed the flutter project within the django project.


## Django
For the rest api, I used [Django Rest Framework]() to provide the api, so I had endpoints such as
```
http://localhost:8000/api/v1/projects
http://localhost:8000/api/v1/tasks
```
If you have used django the `urls.py` file would look as follows

```
urlpatterns = [
	path('api/v1/', include(router.urls)),
]
```
where `router.urls` would be the `ViewSets` for the project and task models.

So once that was running this could be served via the usual `./manage.py runserver`

## Flutter

The development side of UI is very similar to development of building a mobile application in flutter, only major difference is that during development the application can be run via
```
flutter run -d web-server --web-port=9090
```
 and any changes make will be hot reloaded automatically.
 
 
# Integrating with Django.

Once our UI is built, we can build it via

```
flutter build web
```
This will build the flutter application with all the required `javascript`,`icons` and any `asserts` files and placed `build/web` directory which will look simular to the below

```
flutter
    build/
	    web/
		    assets/
			flutter.js
			flutter_service_worker.js
			index.html
			....
```

During the devlopment we were essenailly running 2 services

```
./manage.py runserver
```
and

```
flutter run -d web-server --web-port=9090
```
but now we just want the entire application to be served by django.


## Index file
We can tell django about the index by creating a template view and treating the `index.html` file as a temlate.
We need to first add the file path of `flutter/build/web` to the django templates settings
```
FLUTTER_DIR = BASE_DIR / 'flutter/build/web'
TEMPLATES = [
    ...
	DIRS: [FLUTTE_DIR]
	...
]
```
We can create a template view in our apps `views.py` file

```

class IndexView(TemplateView):
    template_name='index.html'
```

And add it to our `urls.py` file
```
path("app/", IndexView.as_view()),
```

If we browse to `http://localhost:8000/app` we'll be presented with a blank page. This due to the fact that all the static assets(css,js) are relative to the index file and dont follow the djangos way of specifying static files.

So now we need a way to serve this files. We could go in a modify the `index.html` file and change teh urls to `{% static .... %} but then we would need to do that each time we rebuild flutter, we are trying to avoid that path.

## Serving Flutter Static Files

### Django Serve

There's a quicker way to serve the static files from the flutter application.
We can modify our `index.html` file and change `<base href=/>` to `<base href="/app/>`, what this html tag does is, it sets the base url for all relative paths in the index.html file, so `/file.js` would become `/app/file.js`.

With this change we can serve this file in django by matching the appropiate url and serving the static files
```
path("app/<path:resource>', serve_flutter),
```

The `flutter_redirect` function will then serve the file

```
from django.views.static import serve

def serve_flutter(request, resource):
    return serve(request, resource, settings.FLUTTER_DIR)
```

The issue with this is that the `serve` function is only meant to be used for development purposes and not production, we need to find a better way.

### Whitenoise
Whitenoise is a libray that allows a python web application to serve its own static files with out the need of an external service like nginx.
We'll need to make some changes to whitenoise to make it compatible with our app.

